comments.body:
I'm closing this issue, since it is not related to cibuildwheel. Sorry for the fuss.

For information, it was related to ABI differences between builds made with `pip install` and `cibuildwheel`:

- a .so file created with `pip install` uses SYSV (older ABI)
-  a .so file created with `cibuildweel` uses GNU/Linux ABI

So, this issue could be rephrased to two different questions:

* why does cibuildwheel use a different ABI from pip install? 
* why does pip install use the older ABI (SYSV instead of GNU/Linux)

Proof:
```bash
# Below a .so file created with pip install: uses SYSV (older ABI)
> file pi___imgui.cpython-310-x86_64-linux-gnu.so pi___imgui.cpython-310-x86_64-linux-gnu.so:
 ELF 64-bit LSB shared object, x86-64, version 1 (SYSV), dynamically linked

# Below a .so file created with cibuildweel: uses GNU/Linux ABI
> file pixi_imgui.cpython-310-x86_64-linux-gnu.so pixi_imgui.cpython-310-x86_64-linux-gnu.so:
 ELF 64-bit LSB shared object, x86-64, version 1 (GNU/Linux), dynamically linked
```


---

And as for the reason of the segfault, it can be summarized as being a consequence of ImGui using C-style initialization techniques, by zeroing memory. 

`std::string`from the GNU/Linux ABI probably does not support zero-initialization.

```cpp
struct ImGuiContext
{
    ImGuiContext()
    {
        // ImGui uses many C-style initializations techniques, such as this memset
        // However, the std::string provided by GNU/Linux does *not* support to be zero-initialized!!!
        memset(this, 0, sizeof(*this));

        IniFilename = "imgui.ini";
    }
    std::string IniFilename;
};
```
